course: dictionary
lesson: 7084
title: Pax: peace, truce and a Latin word
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Pax is a short word with a big meaning, and the clues around it tell you which meaning fits. It comes from Latin, where it means peace. «After the conquests the Mongol lands were peaceful enough for traders to travel safely across Asia; historians call this the Mongol peace, or Pax Mongolica.» The words peaceful and safely show that pax means a long time of peace.
explain: When pax comes after a name, like Pax Romana, it names a long peace kept by one powerful country. Pax is an uncountable noun, so you cannot count it or make it plural. You say some pax, not two paxes.
explain: In old British school stories, children shouted «Pax!» to stop a fight or game. If a game is getting rough and someone yells pax, they want everyone to stop. Try the swap test: put truce in the sentence instead, and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Pax is the Latin word for peace.
fact: «Pax Romana» means the long peace kept across the Roman Empire.
fact: «Pax Mongolica» means the Mongol peace, when traders could travel safely across Asia.
fact: «Pax!» is old UK school slang that means truce, stop fighting!
fact: In some churches, the pax is a sign or kiss of peace shared between people.
exercise: «After the conquests the Mongol lands were peaceful enough for traders to travel safely across Asia.» What does Pax Mongolica mean? | A Mongol trading card | *The Mongol peace, a long time of peace | The Mongol army, marching to war | Traders who never stop moving
exercise: In an old school story, Tom yells «Pax!» in the middle of a scuffle. What does he mean? | *Truce, stop fighting! | Pack your bag and go home | Pass me the ball | Push harder
exercise: What does «Pax Romana» mean? | Roman food and feasts | Roman soldiers at war | Roman roads built for armies | *A long peace across the Roman Empire
test: Pax is the Latin word for what? | War | Food | Speed | *Peace
test: In «Pax Romana», the word pax means what? | A game of tag | A sharp tool | Money | *Peace
exam: Pax is an uncountable noun. Which is correct? | Pax are coming soon | We need more paxes | *We need more pax | We need three pax
